    "justification": "The finite alternative distinguishes unattainability from high cost without a false polynomial-witness premise.",
    "gap": "A fixed-source decision does not decide existence over the priced physical catalogue.",
    "consumer": "A planner can certify that no legal acquired collection supports the target.",
    "proof_tex": "Let\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{sw}}\n =\\{E_{\\mathrm{sw}}:\\operatorname{Acc}_{\\mathrm{sw}}(Q,E_{\\mathrm{sw}};G)=1\\}.\n\\]\nIf this set is nonempty, Theorem~\\(\\mathrm{Cost\\mbox{-}optimal\\ sparse\\ certificate}\\) returns \\(E_{\\mathrm{sw}}^{\\star}\\) and\n\\[\n \\operatorname{Opt}_{\\mathrm{sw}}\n =\\operatorname{Cost}(E_{\\mathrm{sw}}^{\\star})<+\\infty.\n \\tag{20}\n\\]\nIf it is empty, the convention in Definition~\\(\\mathrm{Switching\\ optimal\\ acquisition\\ cost}\\) gives \\(\\operatorname{Opt}_{\\mathrm{sw}}=+\\infty\\).  These cases are disjoint and exhaustive.\n\nThe weighted-cover identity gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\ne\\varnothing\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\bigcup_{S\\in\\Pi}K_{\\mathrm{sw}}(S)=\\Omega.\n \\tag{21}\n\\]\nLemma~\\(\\mathrm{Sparse\\ canonical\\ certificate\\ bound}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\ne\\varnothing\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\exists E_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\in\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{sw}}\n \\text{ with }N_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\leq B_{\\mathrm{sw}},\n \\tag{22}\n\\]\nand Theorem~\\(\\mathrm{Sparse\\ switching\\ source\\ normal\\ form}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{sw}}\\ne\\varnothing\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n \\exists E_0\\text{ finite and correctly typed such that the complete run on }\n \\mathcal R(E_0)\\text{ returns nonfailure}.\n \\tag{23}\n\\]\nNegating (21)--(23) proves all infeasibility equivalences.\n\nConstructing the incidence and running the faster of the two exact cover algorithms has the query and time bounds in (19).  It decides whether the union in (21) is all of \\(\\Omega\\), so it decides the alternative.  In the negative case there is no output certificate to encode.  Because (21)--(23) compare operational predicates only, their negations do not assert semantic nonidentifiability.",
